# Detailed Comments on Functionality

When adding or modifying production code, document **what it does, why it exists, and non-obvious behavior** before or alongside the implementation.

## What to Comment

| Layer | Required documentation |
|-------|------------------------|
| **Module** | One-line purpose at top; note upstream/downstream role (summaries → PropBet → insights → trends table) |
| **Class** | Responsibility, dependencies injected, and what callers should expect |
| **Public methods / functions** | Docstring: purpose, parameters, return value, edge cases (no flag fired, missing hit rate, split market) |
| **Non-obvious logic** | Inline comment explaining the business rule (e.g. 70% hot threshold, recommended_side selection) |

## What Not to Comment

- Test files (behavior is described by test names and assertions)
- Trivial one-liners that restate the code (`i += 1  # increment i`)
- Generated or boilerplate config

## Python Style

Use docstrings for classes and public functions; use inline comments for branching logic and domain rules.

```python
# ✅ GOOD — module + class + method documented
"""MLB insight evaluation for a single PropBet.

Runs _check_* detectors and renders a headline when last-10-game hit rate is hot.
Does not query the DB — TrendGetter supplies PropBet.
"""

def _check_ten_game_hit_rate_is_hot(self, prop: PropBet) -> FlagResult | None:
    """Return a hot-side flag when ten_game rate >= 0.70 at the bet line.

    Returns None when rate is missing or below threshold.
    hits is derived as round(rate * 10) for template copy.
    """
```

```python
# ❌ BAD — no docstring, magic threshold unexplained
def _check_ten_game_hit_rate_is_hot(self, prop):
    if prop.rates.ten_game >= 0.70:
        ...
```

## Review Checklist

Before finishing a change, confirm:

- [ ] New modules and classes have purpose documented
- [ ] Every new public method/function has a docstring with edge cases
- [ ] Domain rules (hit-rate thresholds, side selection, headline merge) are explained in comments
- [ ] Comments describe **why**, not just **what** the next line does
